#X text 620 16 This whole setup is simple and complicated at the same time. The labels used in the Help patches to show you how it works are used in a way that are meant to convey the data they are used for. In fact \, you can name them just about anything and they should work. The part I was not getting for the receiving of data from the canvas object was that you have to have the label set up for the canvas send property as well. i like to keep the send and receive the same as I find it simpler that way. I might change my mind later. Regarding the delta proprerty for canvas \, it appears that it changes the x and y positions for that canvas but for every change that is made \, it translates the canvas by that amount. So when the values start to get larger \, the canvas really starts to jump around by the larger amounts. The position does not work the same way as given here because the values are changing by increments of 1 in sequence. This happens either by the sliders changing the values or changing the numbers in the float boxes or number atoms if they are used. The use of the position sliders are useful to bring the canvas back into frame because those values used are not relative \, they are fixed positions on and x-y axis. When using plugdata \, you can zoom out and there is a map shown of the whole useable patch area and you can navigate it and find the canvas but it is easier here to use the pos (position) property. I do not know why the examples tend to use a $ before the send and receive labels though.;
#X text 627 395 Sometimes a change of environment can help in learning. I started to explore this part of the patches in plugdata on my iPad. And because I can zoom out \, I realized how big the distances were of the changes from delta.;
